From fcc1663aba33f71feac10a711a21866860f8d344 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 14:37:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] feat(workflow-runtime): execute runs in the background
 and recover them

The reference runtime only recorded runs: start() moved a run to Running
and nothing ever executed its steps, so every run stayed Running forever
and cancel(cleanup=True) compensated steps that had never run.

LocalWorkflowRuntime (forgemesh.workflowruntime.runtime) executes each
started run in a task it owns, using the existing WorkflowExecutor
(retries, step and workflow timeouts), CompensationExecutor and
ApprovalGate, and persists every step transition and result:

- all steps succeed -> Succeeded; a step that exhausts its retries (or
  the workflow timeout) -> Compensating, completed steps compensated in
  reverse order, unexecuted steps Skipped, then Failed with an error;
- cancel() interrupts the running step and waits for Cancelled,
  compensating completed steps when cleanup=True;
- recover() resumes unfinished runs after a restart from the first step
  that did not succeed, or finishes an interrupted compensation;
- start() stays idempotent on its key and get_run()/find_run() return
  snapshots with step results;
- aclose() cancels or awaits every task so no task outlives the runtime.

The control plane cannot import plugin modules, so the engine lives in
the workflow-runtime package and ReferenceWorkflowRuntime becomes a thin
subclass. WorkflowStore serialises its connection with a lock (runs now
write concurrently from worker threads), enables WAL like the control
plane database, records a run error (adding the column to old files),
keeps step results through compensation, counts attempts and looks runs
up by idempotency key. Conformance scenarios now run in one event loop.
